feat: fuller chapters, platform symbols, and an artefact that crosses the frame

The chapters stated their numbers and stopped. They now carry what is actually
behind them.

STEAM shows both accounts — avatar, handle, online state and current game —
so the five thousand hours visibly belong to one person with two logins
holding different halves of it, rather than to an abstract total.

PLAYSTATION gets its own alphabet: the four face-button marks say "console"
faster than any label and, unlike key art, they are the platform's signature
rather than someone else's poster. Each trophy tier gains a cup drawn in its
own metal, so the tier reads before the number does.

PRESENCE becomes the profile as Discord itself presents it: banner, avatar
decoration, bio, badges and the account's own accent. That accent is allowed
past the one-cold-ramp rule for a reason rather than as an exception —
Discord's blurple already sits inside the ramp, which is the whole thesis the
palette was built on.

The artefact no longer parks on the right for the entire middle. It is now
choreographed against the type: right while STEAM holds the left rail, then
across to the left once PLAYSTATION mirrors to the right rail, right again and
further away for the quiet chapter, and finally centred and closest of all for
the ending. Alternating sides is also the only way it can move left without
landing underneath the copy — the crossing and the mirrored layout are one
decision, not two.
